//! datara-studio :: the built-in explorer
//!
//! A directory browser, in Datara, for opening a project folder. Written here
//! rather than delegated to a native dialog because browsing the filesystem is
//! the one thing this IDE needs from the platform, and doing it in the kernel's
//! own language means one implementation serves Windows, Linux and macOS
//! instead of one per platform.
//!
//! Cross-platform by asking the environment rather than the compiler: Windows
//! always sets `SystemRoot` and Unix never does. Measured, not assumed - see
//! `st_is_windows`.
//!
//! # Rewritten for forgen 1.4.0: the shell is gone from the listing path
//!
//! The first version asked the platform shell for everything - `cd` to test a
//! directory, `dir /b /s` to list a tree, `dir /b /ad` to list folders. That
//! worked, and it cost three things that are all now fixed by calling the
//! runtime instead:
//!
//!   * **`dir /b /s` from a drive root walked the entire disk.** Measured: the
//!     server grew to **3.9 GB** and stopped answering, and the interface showed
//!     "workspace unreadable" with no way out. The response cap could not help,
//!     because the listing had to finish before anything could be capped. The
//!     workaround was to list a drive root one level deep and report
//!     `shallow: true`. **`dir_list` is native, so the walk is bounded as it
//!     goes** and the drive root needs no special case at all: `st_tree` carries
//!     a budget and stops, whether the root is `D:/` or a project folder.
//!   * **`/api/tree` cost 487 ms** - two shell listings, two process starts.
//!     Native `dir_list` is a directory read, and the tree is now walked in
//!     process.
//!   * **`cd` was the only reliable directory test**, because `file_exists`
//!     returns false for a folder. `path_exists` answers for a folder, a file, a
//!     missing path and a drive root alike, so `st_dir_exists` is now
//!     `path_exists && !file_exists` instead of a process launch.
//!
//! Three things still need the shell, and they are named rather than hidden:
//! creating a directory, moving a file, and content search. forgen 1.4.0 has no
//! builtin for any of the three - `dir_list`, `path_exists`, `file_exists`,
//! `file_read`, `file_read_bytes`, `file_write`, `file_append`,
//! `file_write_bytes` are the whole filesystem surface. So `st_sh` survives for
//! exactly `st_mkdir`, `st_move` and `st_find`, and each of those verifies its
//! own result by looking at the filesystem rather than by reading the shell's
//! message, because that message is translated.
//!
//! # The encoding, and the one defect that is not ours
//!
//! Text that comes out of the shell is console-codepage bytes (cp866 on a
//! Russian Windows). Text that comes out of the **native** builtins is **ANSI**
//! bytes (cp1251), and that is a runtime defect with a precise cause:
//! `src/runtime/datara_runtime.c:2623` calls `FindFirstFileA` with a
//! `WIN32_FIND_DATAA`, so the ANSI Win32 API hands back cp1251 and a name
//! outside the ANSI codepage cannot be represented at all. Measured: the folder
//! `тест папка 123` comes back as the fourteen bytes
//! `242 229 241 242 32 239 224 239 234 224 32 49 50 51`, which is cp1251, and
//! `validate_utf8` on it is false.
//!
//! The fix belongs in the runtime: `FindFirstFileW` plus
//! `WideCharToMultiByte(CP_UTF8, ...)`. When that lands, `st_from_ansi` and its
//! two tables are deleted and nothing else changes. Until then both conversions
//! exist, each in one place, and `st_from_ansi` is the one the listing path
//! uses.

use http

/// The marker every shell call echoes on success.
///
/// Success is decided by this string appearing, never by matching error text,
/// because error text is translated and the translation is not ours to guess.
pub fn st_ok_mark() -> Str {
    return "__DS_OK__"
}

/// Where the shell script and its capture live.
///
/// A temporary directory rather than the workspace: a failed move must not leave
/// a script in the project a person is trying to clean up.
///
/// On Windows `TEMP` is the name the platform sets, and it is the only one of the
/// three candidates the runtime reports on this machine (measured: `TEMP`,
/// `TMPDIR` and `TMP` all answer `C:\Temp` here). `TMP` is deliberately not
/// consulted as a second choice: the script is invoked by a constant string and
/// the two have to agree, so this function and `st_sh` both ask `TEMP` and both
/// fall back to the same literal. On Unix the answer is `/tmp`, for the same
/// reason - the literal in `st_sh` says `/tmp` and there is no expanding `%TEMP%`
/// there to keep them in step.
fn st_shell_temp() -> Str {
    if !st_is_windows() {
        return "/tmp"
    }
    let t = str_trim(env_get("TEMP"))
    if byte_len(t) == 0 {
        return "C:/Temp"
    }
    return t
}

/// The fixed capture file for shell output. The server is single-threaded, so one
/// file is enough and avoids a second process just to invent a random name.
pub fn st_shell_capture() -> Str {
    return st_join(st_shell_temp(), "datara-studio-shell.log")
}

/// Where the shell script records the exit code of the command it ran.
///
/// Separate from the output file on purpose: output and status are different
/// facts and a caller that wants one should not have to parse the other.
/// `st_sh` writes it as a side effect of every call and ignores it; `st_run`
/// reads it, because the compiler's real exit code is the thing it reports.
pub fn st_code_capture() -> Str {
    return st_join(st_shell_temp(), "datara-studio-code.log")
}

/// The exit code of the most recent `st_sh` command, or -1 if it is unknown.
///
/// -1 rather than 0 for "unknown", because 0 means success and a missing file
/// must never be read as one.
pub fn st_last_code() -> Int {
    mut v = ""
    unsafe(justification: "the explorer reads the exit code its own shell script recorded") {
        if file_exists(st_code_capture()) {
            v = str_trim(file_read(st_code_capture()))
        }
    }
    if byte_len(v) == 0 {
        return -1
    }
    return str_to_int(v)
}

/// The script the command is written into. See `st_sh` - this is SEAM-1.
fn st_shell_script() -> Str {
    if st_is_windows() {
        return st_join(st_shell_temp(), "datara-studio-shell.bat")
    }
    return st_join(st_shell_temp(), "datara-studio-shell.sh")
}

/// Run a command through the platform shell and return stdout and stderr
/// together.
///
/// ## SEAM-1 - the command travels as a file, not as an argument
///
/// **forgen 1.4.2 corrupts any string built at run time on its way into
/// `system()`, `exec()` and `process_output()`, and it does so under AOT only.**
/// Measured with an identical 57-byte string: `forgen run` (JIT) executes it and
/// returns 0, while the built binary hands cmd a mangled line and cmd answers
/// `"7" is not an internal or external command`. A literal is unaffected, and so
/// is a concatenation the compiler can fold; anything that reads a variable is
/// not. Four shapes were probed - inline, into a `let`, through a helper, and via
/// `str_join` - and all four failed, with `system()` returning a pointer
/// (`3022831024624`) where the exit code belongs.
///
/// That is the whole of "перетаскивать в папку не получается" and "ворктрее не
/// вижу". `move`, `mkdir`, `del`, `git` and the project search all funnel through
/// this one function, so in the shipped server every one of them silently did
/// nothing while the endpoint reported success. It is also why the earlier
/// `exec` -> `system` change did not help: both primitives have the defect, and
/// the old comment blaming `exec` was describing the symptom, not the cause.
///
/// The way round it is to stop passing the command *as an argument*. It is
/// written into a script, and the script is invoked by a **compile-time literal**
/// - the one thing this AOT path passes through intact. `%TEMP%` is expanded by
/// cmd itself, so the literal stays constant while the directory is still
/// whatever the machine actually uses. Output comes back through a file, which is
/// what the callers already expected, and a script also removes the nested-quote
/// hazard: nothing here has to survive being re-parsed by a second shell.
///
/// Do not "tidy" this back into one `system("cmd /c " + command)`. It compiles,
/// it passes every structure test, and it does nothing.
///
/// One seam on purpose. When forgen stops corrupting the argument, the body of
/// this function is the only thing that has to change.
pub fn st_sh( command: Str) -> Str {
    return st_sh_with_env("", command)
}

/// `st_sh` with a preamble of `set` lines, for the one caller that has to hand
/// the child an environment.
///
/// ## Why the environment is set in the child and not with `env_set`
///
/// Because `env_set` cannot take a value this program built.
///
/// `tc_prepend_msvc` used to stage `env_set("PATH", bin + ";" + env_get("PATH"))`
/// - a concatenation - and forgen 1.4.2 corrupts a run-time-built value on its
/// way into `env_set`. Measured, with three shapes side by side:
///
///     env_set("T3", "MARK;")              -> child saw  "MARK;"
///     env_set("T4", env_get("PATH"))      -> child saw  2656 bytes, intact
///     env_set("T1", "MARK;" + env_get("PATH"))  -> the child did not run at all
///
/// and a 1500-character string built in a loop reached the child as one garbage
/// byte. The consequence was not a wrong PATH, it was an unusable one: `git`,
/// `python` and `cmd.exe` all stopped resolving, so the tree could not be read,
/// the worktree chip disappeared and every shell-driven feature answered with
/// the shell's own complaint pasted into a field that expected a path. That is
/// the screenshot: EXPLORER reading "cannot read that folder - not a folder:
/// \"git\" is not recognized as an internal or external command".
///
/// `cmd` does the concatenation correctly, so the concatenation is moved into
/// `cmd`. The preamble is written into the script file, which is the one route
/// that carries a run-time string intact - the same route SEAM-1 uses.
///
/// The asymmetry in the `%` handling below is deliberate. The preamble is
/// written by this program and *needs* `%PATH%` to expand, so it is passed
/// through untouched. The command carries paths that came from the user, so a
/// literal `%` in one of them is doubled and stays literal.
pub fn st_sh_with_env( env: Str, command: Str) -> Str {
    let capture = st_shell_capture()
    let script = st_shell_script()
    // Empty the capture before the command runs, so that a failure to write it
    // reads as "no output" instead of as the previous command's answer.
    //
    // This is defence, not decoration. A long-lived child that inherits the
    // capture as its stdout keeps the file open, and a redirect that cannot be
    // written leaves the old contents in place - which is how `mkdir` once
    // reported the companion's startup banner as its own error message. The
    // launch no longer shares this file (`st_ai_launch`), and this line is what
    // stops the next such mistake from being silent.
    unsafe(justification: "the explorer clears its own capture file before the shell writes it") {
        file_write(capture, "")
    }
    unsafe(justification: "the explorer writes the shell command it is about to run") {
        if st_is_windows() {
            // A batch file expands `%`, so a folder genuinely named `100% done`
            // would lose its tail on the way through - and the path comes from
            // the user, not from here. Doubling the sign is how batch spells a
            // literal percent. This is the price of the script-file route and it
            // is paid in one place.
            //
            // The exit code is recorded on its own line rather than inside the
            // parenthesised group. Batch expands `%VAR%` when it *reads* a line,
            // and a line inside `( ... )` is read as part of the whole group -
            // so `%ERRORLEVEL%` written inside the parens would be the value
            // from before the command ran. On its own line it is read after, and
            // it is the code the command actually returned. `st_last_code` reads
            // it; `st_sh` itself ignores it.
            file_write(script, "@echo off\r\n" + env + "\r\n("
                             + str_replace(command, "%", "%%") + ") > \""
                             + st_back_path(capture) + "\" 2>&1\r\n"
                             + "echo %ERRORLEVEL% > \""
                             + st_back_path(st_code_capture()) + "\"\r\n")
        } else {
            file_write(script, "#!/bin/sh\n" + env + "\n(" + command + ") > \""
                             + capture + "\" 2>&1\n"
                             + "echo $? > \"" + st_code_capture() + "\"\n")
        }
    }
    if st_is_windows() {
        // **The shell is named by absolute path, and that is a fix, not a
        // precaution.**
        //
        // The studio edits its own PATH: `tc_prepend_msvc` stages
        // `env_set("PATH", bin + ";" + env_get("PATH"))`. forgen 1.4.2 corrupts
        // a run-time-built value on its way into `env_set` - measured: a
        // 1500-character string built in a loop arrived at the child as a single
        // garbage byte - so the child's PATH comes out unusable and `cmd.exe`
        // cannot be resolved by name. Measured directly: with that prepend
        // staged, a child answers
        //
        //     "cmd.exe" is not recognized as an internal or external command
        //
        // and every `system("cmd.exe /d /c ...")` in this file silently does
        // nothing at all - the script is written, the folder is never created,
        // the capture stays empty. That single fact is why the tree could not be
        // dragged into, why the worktree chip was missing and why the companion
        // switch looked dead, all at once and only after `/api/health` had been
        // called once.
        //
        // The bridge must not be breakable by the environment this program
        // itself writes, so the shell is named absolutely. Each invocation
        // below is still a compile-time literal, so SEAM-1 holds; only the
        // choice between them is made at run time.
        let has_temp = byte_len(str_trim(env_get("TEMP"))) > 0
        let abs_cmd = st_file_exists("C:/Windows/System32/cmd.exe")
        unsafe(justification: "the explorer runs its own shell script") {
            if abs_cmd {
                if has_temp {
                    system("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe /d /c \"%TEMP%\\datara-studio-shell.bat\"")
                } else {
                    system("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe /d /c \"C:\\Temp\\datara-studio-shell.bat\"")
                }
            } else {
                if has_temp {
                    system("cmd.exe /d /c \"%TEMP%\\datara-studio-shell.bat\"")
                } else {
                    system("cmd.exe /d /c \"C:\\Temp\\datara-studio-shell.bat\"")
                }
            }
        }
    } else {
        unsafe(justification: "the explorer runs its own shell script") {
            system("sh /tmp/datara-studio-shell.sh")
        }
    }
    mut res = ""
    unsafe(justification: "the explorer reads the shell result from its temporary capture") {
        if file_exists(capture) {
            res = file_read(capture)
        }
    }
    return res
}

/// The script the companion launch is written into.
///
/// A separate file from `st_shell_script`, and that separation is the whole
/// point - see `st_ai_launch`.
fn st_ai_script() -> Str {
    if st_is_windows() {
        return st_join(st_shell_temp(), "datara-studio-ai.bat")
    }
    return st_join(st_shell_temp(), "datara-studio-ai.sh")
}

/// Start a detached process with **nothing redirected**.
///
/// ## Why this is not `st_sh`
///
/// It was, for exactly one build, and the regression is worth recording because
/// it is the kind that looks like five unrelated bugs.
///
/// `st_sh` wraps its command as `( ... ) > capture 2>&1`, and `start "" /b`
/// gives the child the *current* standard handles. So the daemon inherited the
/// capture file as its stdout and went on holding it for the rest of its life -
/// which, for a detached daemon, is until the machine restarts. From that moment
/// every shell feature in the IDE was broken, and each one failed differently:
/// `st_port_busy` read the daemon's startup banner instead of netstat's output
/// and therefore reported "nothing is listening on 7890" while the companion was
/// plainly listening on 7890; `mkdir` answered "could not create the folder"
/// with the daemon's banner as its detail; `git` answered "not a git
/// repository". One long-lived child had taken the single file the entire shell
/// bridge reads through.
///
/// So the launch gets its own script and **no redirection at all**. The child
/// inherits the server's own console, which costs nothing, and the shared
/// capture stays the property of commands that finish.
///
/// The invocation is still a compile-time literal, so SEAM-1 still applies: the
/// command is written to a file rather than passed as an argument.
pub fn st_ai_launch( command: Str) -> Int {
    let script = st_ai_script()
    unsafe(justification: "the IDE writes the script that starts its own optional companion") {
        if st_is_windows() {
            file_write(script, "@echo off\r\n" + str_replace(command, "%", "%%") + "\r\n")
        } else {
            file_write(script, "#!/bin/sh\n" + command + "\n")
        }
    }
    mut code = -1
    if st_is_windows() {
        // Named absolutely for the same measured reason `st_sh` is - see the
        // long note there. This one matters twice over, because the companion
        // launch is the first thing the interface does on load, so it is also
        // the first thing that would fail.
        let has_temp = byte_len(str_trim(env_get("TEMP"))) > 0
        let abs_cmd = st_file_exists("C:/Windows/System32/cmd.exe")
        unsafe(justification: "the IDE starts its own optional companion process") {
            if abs_cmd {
                if has_temp {
                    code = system("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe /d /c \"%TEMP%\\datara-studio-ai.bat\"")
                } else {
                    code = system("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe /d /c \"C:\\Temp\\datara-studio-ai.bat\"")
                }
            } else {
                if has_temp {
                    code = system("cmd.exe /d /c \"%TEMP%\\datara-studio-ai.bat\"")
                } else {
                    code = system("cmd.exe /d /c \"C:\\Temp\\datara-studio-ai.bat\"")
                }
            }
        }
    } else {
        unsafe(justification: "the IDE starts its own optional companion process") {
            code = system("sh /tmp/datara-studio-ai.sh")
        }
    }
    return code
}

/// Is something already *listening* on a loopback port?
///
/// There is no client-side socket builtin - the runtime has create, bind,
/// listen, accept, recv, send and close, and nothing that connects - so this asks
/// the operating system instead. One `netstat -an` is a process launch, about
/// 220 ms on this machine, which is why it is only on the path where a person has
/// explicitly asked for something to be started.
///
/// Why it exists at all. The runtime does not refuse a second bind on a port, so
/// every "start the companion" that arrived while one was already running started
/// another one, and every one of them bound 7890 without error. Measured on this
/// machine: **115 python processes, 2.64 GB resident**, about thirty of them
/// listening on that single port. A request then went to whichever one Windows
/// happened to pick, so the companion's answers were not reproducible between two
/// identical keystrokes - which from the outside is "the suggestions are unclear".
///
/// ## Why the answer has to be "is any line LISTENING", not "is the port string here"
///
/// The first version searched the whole `netstat -an` output for `":7890 "`. That
/// substring appears in **every** row for the port, not just the listening one:
/// an established connection carries it, and so does a `TIME_WAIT` row - which
/// Windows keeps for two minutes after the peer closes, and which therefore
/// outlives the process that owned it by a long way.
///
/// The visible effect was the opposite of the intent: the companion had exited
/// (or been stopped), a `TIME_WAIT` row was still on screen, this answered "yes,
/// something is listening", `st_ai_start` returned `{"already":true}` and started
/// nothing, and the IDE then reported the companion as running while every
/// `/health` failed. Restarting the editor did not help, because the row was
/// still there - so the companion looked like it could not be started at all.
///
/// Matching on the state word is the honest test. `LISTENING` is the only state
/// that means "a process is waiting for connections here", and it is the one
/// `st_ai_start` actually cares about.
///
/// --- and the whole function was Windows-only, which made it worse than useless
/// ---
///
/// On Linux and macOS this returned **false for every port, always**, because
/// nothing in it holds on either platform:
///
///   * the state word is `LISTEN`, not `LISTENING` - the `ing` is a Windows
///     spelling;
///   * macOS writes the port with a dot, `127.0.0.1.7890`, so the `":7890 "`
///     needle matches no macOS row even after the state is fixed;
///   * and `netstat` itself is from net-tools, which is deprecated and absent
///     from a minimal Linux install, so on plenty of machines the command is not
///     there to run at all.
///
/// The consequence is the exact failure described above, running backwards:
/// `st_ai_start` saw "nothing is listening", started another companion, and did
/// it on every call. The 115-process, 2.64 GB incident was found on Windows and
/// fixed there; on Linux it was still waiting, and it is not visible from a
/// Windows machine because this path is correct on the platform it was written
/// for.
///
/// So the Unix half does not parse an answer, it asks the question. Three tools
/// in order of preference - `lsof` (default on macOS), `ss` (the current Linux
/// tool), then `netstat` for anything older - each asked "is something listening
/// on this port", and the success marker printed only if one of them says yes.
/// No column layout, no state word, no dependency on net-tools being installed.
/// `grep` is not a portability risk; it is in POSIX and on every Unix here.
pub fn st_port_busy( port: Int) -> Bool {
    if !st_is_windows() {
        let p = int_to_str(port)
        let probe = "( ( command -v lsof >/dev/null 2>&1"
                  + " && lsof -nP -iTCP:" + p + " -sTCP:LISTEN >/dev/null 2>&1 )"
                  + " || ( command -v ss >/dev/null 2>&1"
                  + " && ss -ltn 2>/dev/null | grep -q \":" + p + " \" )"
                  + " || ( command -v netstat >/dev/null 2>&1"
                  + " && netstat -an 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"[.:]" + p + " .*LISTEN\" )"
                  + " ) && echo " + st_ok_mark()
        let seen = st_sh(probe)
        if str_index_of(seen, st_ok_mark()) >= 0 {
            return true
        }
        return false
    }

    let out = st_sh("netstat -an")
    // Look for the port and the state on the SAME line, rather than searching
    // for the port anywhere in the output.
    //
    // `":7890 "` still has to be the needle - the trailing space is what stops
    // `:7890` matching `:78900`, and netstat pads every address field so the
    // space is always there. The second needle is the state.
    let needle = ":" + int_to_str(port) + " "
    mut rest = out
    // No `if` expressions in this language, so the line is taken in two steps
    // rather than assigned from a conditional.
    mut line = ""
    mut nl = 0
    while byte_len(rest) > 0 {
        nl = str_index_of(rest, "\n")
        if nl < 0 {
            line = rest
        } else {
            line = str_substring(rest, 0, nl)
        }
        if str_index_of(line, needle) >= 0 {
            // netstat writes the state in upper case on every locale this has
            // been run in, and `LISTENING` survives the codepage conversion
            // because it is pure ASCII - unlike the header, which is localised.
            if str_index_of(line, "LISTENING") >= 0 {
                return true
            }
        }
        if nl < 0 {
            rest = ""
        } else {
            rest = str_substring(rest, nl + 1, byte_len(rest) - nl - 1)
        }
    }
    return false
}

/// The last whitespace-separated field of a line.
///
/// `str_split` on a single space produces an empty string for every run of
/// spaces, and `netstat` pads its columns, so the fields have to be filtered
/// rather than indexed. Taking the last non-empty one is what makes this
/// independent of the column widths.
fn st_last_field( line: Str) -> Str {
    let parts = str_split(str_trim(line), " ")
    let n = parts.len()
    mut last = ""
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let p = str_trim(parts[i])
        if byte_len(p) > 0 {
            last = p
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return last
}

/// The process id of whatever is LISTENING on a loopback port, or `""`.
///
/// Needed because the companion is started detached - by `wscript` on Windows and
/// by a backgrounded subshell elsewhere - so there is no child handle to wait on
/// and no pid file to read. The port is the only thing this program knows about
/// it, and `netstat -ano` is the only place the owner is written down. It is the
/// same command `st_port_busy` already runs, for the same reason.
///
/// The pid is the last field of the row, not a fixed column: the local and remote
/// address fields are padded to widths that depend on the address family, so
/// counting characters lands in the wrong place as soon as an IPv6 row appears.
///
/// The `LISTENING` test is the same one `st_port_busy` makes and for the same
/// reason - a `TIME_WAIT` row for the port outlives its owner by two minutes, and
/// killing the pid on one of those would kill an unrelated process that has since
/// been handed the number.
pub fn st_pid_on_port( port: Int) -> Str {
    let p = int_to_str(port)
    if !st_is_windows() {
        // `-t` is "terse": one pid per line, nothing else. That is the whole
        // point of asking lsof rather than parsing its table.
        let out = st_sh("lsof -nP -iTCP:" + p + " -sTCP:LISTEN -t 2>/dev/null")
        let lines = str_split(out, "\n")
        let n = lines.len()
        mut i = 0
        while i < n {
            let t = str_trim(lines[i])
            i = i + 1
            if byte_len(t) > 0 {
                return t
            }
        }
        return ""
    }
    let out = st_sh("netstat -ano")
    let needle = ":" + p + " "
    mut rest = out
    mut nl = 0
    while byte_len(rest) > 0 {
        nl = str_index_of(rest, "\n")
        mut line = ""
        if nl < 0 {
            line = rest
        } else {
            line = str_substring(rest, 0, nl)
        }
        if str_index_of(line, needle) >= 0 {
            if str_index_of(line, "LISTENING") >= 0 {
                return st_last_field(line)
            }
        }
        if nl < 0 {
            rest = ""
        } else {
            rest = str_substring(rest, nl + 1, byte_len(rest) - nl - 1)
        }
    }
    return ""
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------- cp1251 -> UTF-8
//
// The native builtins return ANSI bytes. Two tables and one walker cover the
// whole codepage: `st_ansi_lo` is 0x80..0xBF and `st_ansi_hi` is 0xC0..0xFF,
// whose 64 Cyrillic letters are contiguous and in order.
//
// 0x98 has no cp1251 mapping and 0xAD is a soft hyphen, which is invisible in a
// filename; both are rendered as `?` so a name containing one still reads as a
// name instead of collapsing.

/// cp1251 0x80..0xBF, one character per position.
fn st_ansi_lo() -> Str {
    return "ЂЃ‚ѓ„…†‡€‰Љ‹ЊЌЋЏ"
         + "ђ‘’“”•–—?™љ›њќћџ"
         + " ЎўЈ¤Ґ¦§Ё©Є«¬?®Ї"
         + "°±Ііґµ¶·ё№є»јЅѕї"
}

/// cp1251 0xC0..0xFF: А..Я then а..я.
fn st_ansi_hi() -> Str {
    return "АБВГДЕЖЗИЙКЛМНОПРСТУФХЦЧШЩЪЫЬЭЮЯ"
         + "абвгдежзийклмнопрстуфхцчшщъыьэюя"
}

/// The character at position `idx` of a table.
///
/// By walking character boundaries, not by arithmetic: `str_substring` indexes
/// bytes and the table mixes two-byte Cyrillic with three-byte box drawing, so
/// `idx * 2` would land in the middle of a character.
fn st_char_idx( table: Str, idx: Int) -> Str {
    let total = byte_len(table)
    mut i = 0
    mut n = 0
    while n < idx && i < total {
        let j = str_next_offset(table, i)
        if j <= i {
            i = total
        } else {
            i = j
        }
        n = n + 1
    }
    if i >= total {
        return "?"
    }
    let j2 = str_next_offset(table, i)
    mut width = j2 - i
    if width <= 0 {
        width = 1
    }
    return str_substring(table, i, width)
}

fn st_has_high_byte( s: Str) -> Bool {
    let n = byte_len(s)
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        if str_byte_at(s, i) > 127 {
            return true
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return false
}

/// Translate ANSI text from a native builtin into UTF-8.
///
/// The fast path matters: the workspace tree is mostly ASCII, and returning the
/// string untouched when no high byte is present means the tree poll costs
/// nothing for the usual case.
pub fn st_from_ansi( s: Str) -> Str {
    if !st_is_windows() {
        return s
    }
    if !st_has_high_byte(s) {
        return s
    }
    mut parts: List<Str> = []
    let n = byte_len(s)
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let b = str_byte_at(s, i)
        if b < 128 {
            parts = parts.push(str_substring(s, i, 1))
        } else {
            if b < 192 {
                parts = parts.push(st_char_idx(st_ansi_lo(), b - 128))
            } else {
                parts = parts.push(st_char_idx(st_ansi_hi(), b - 192))
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    // joined rather than concatenated in the loop: `+` in a loop is quadratic,
    // and a tree listing can be a few hundred kilobytes
    return str_join(parts, "")
}

/// A string that is guaranteed to be valid UTF-8, for anything that becomes JSON.
///
/// Two kinds of text reach this module and only one of them is UTF-8:
///
///   * what the browser sent, which is already UTF-8;
///   * what the runtime's filesystem builtins and the shell handed back, which
///     is the ANSI codepage (cp1251) or the console codepage (cp866).
///
/// A raw cp1251 path inside a JSON string makes the whole document invalid: a
/// parser is entitled to reject it and a browser shows mojibake. So a value that
/// is already correct is never converted twice - `validate_utf8` decides which
/// case it is. Measured on the check endpoint: the `path` field was correct
/// while the `cmd` field, which echoed the raw path, carried `f2 e5 f1 f2` for
/// `тест`.
pub fn st_display( s: Str) -> Str {
    if !st_has_high_byte(s) {
        return s
    }
    mut ok = true
    unsafe(justification: "the IDE must not put invalid UTF-8 into a JSON reply") {
        ok = validate_utf8(s)
    }
    if ok {
        return s
    }
    return st_from_ansi(s)
}

/// Like `st_display`, but for text that came back from a shell.
///
/// Two writers share one output stream and they do not agree on an encoding.
/// `cmd` writes the console codepage (cp866) for its own messages; a program it
/// launched writes whatever its runtime uses, and forgen writes UTF-8. Because
/// the output is redirected to a *file* rather than a pipe, nothing transcodes it
/// on the way, so the buffer is a mix.
///
/// Validity is the only rule that handles both: correct UTF-8 is left alone, and
/// anything else is read as cp866. Measured: reading forgen's own diagnostics as
/// cp866 turned the folder `тест папка 123` into `╤В╨╡╤Б╤В ╨┐╨░╨┐╨║╨░ 123`, which is
/// exactly those UTF-8 bytes seen through cp866.
pub fn st_display_shell( s: Str) -> Str {
    if !st_has_high_byte(s) {
        return s
    }
    mut ok = true
    unsafe(justification: "the IDE must not put invalid UTF-8 into a JSON reply") {
        ok = validate_utf8(s)
    }
    if ok {
        return s
    }
    return st_from_oem(s)
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ cp866 -> UTF-8
//
// Shell output only. `findstr` prints paths, and a failure prints the shell's
// own message; both arrive in the console codepage.

/// The OEM codepage's upper half, 0x80..0xFE, in order.
///
/// `exec("dir /b /ad \"D:/\")` returns the folder "Шахматная школа 64 линии" as
/// the bytes 152 160 229 172 160 226 173 160 239 32 ... That is **cp866**, the
/// OEM codepage of a console on a Russian Windows. The runtime keeps whatever
/// the child wrote, byte for byte, and `str_len` counts those bytes - so the
/// string is not UTF-8, and the explorer showed mojibake.
///
/// `chcp 65001` does **not** fix it: measured with and without, the bytes are
/// identical, because cmd writes a redirected pipe in the OEM codepage
/// regardless of the console codepage.
///
/// 0xFF is a non-breaking space in cp866 and is handled in `st_from_oem`
/// instead of here, because a literal NBSP in source is invisible and unsafe to
/// edit. Regenerate the table with:
///
///   python -c "print('АБВГДЕЖЗИЙКЛМНОПРСТУФХЦЧШЩЪЫЬЭЮЯ' \
///     'абвгдежзийклмноп' '░▒▓│┤╡╢╖╕╣║╗╝╜╛┐' '└┴┬├─┼╞╟╚╔╩╦╠═╬╧' \
///     '╨╤╥╙╘╒╓╫╪┘┌█▄▌▐▀' 'рстуфхцчшщъыьэюя' 'ЁёЄєЇїЎў°∙·√№¤■')"
fn st_oem_table() -> Str {
    return "АБВГДЕЖЗИЙКЛМНОПРСТУФХЦЧ"
         + "ШЩЪЫЬЭЮЯабвгдежзийклмноп"
         + "░▒▓│┤╡╢╖╕╣║╗╝╜╛┐└┴┬├─┼╞╟"
         + "╚╔╩╦╠═╬╧╨╤╥╙╘╒╓╫╪┘┌█▄▌▐▀"
         + "рстуфхцчшщъыьэюяЁёЄєЇїЎў"
         + "°∙·√№¤■"
}

/// Translate OEM-encoded text from the shell into UTF-8.
///
/// Used for `findstr` output and for the shell's own error text. `st_mkdir` and
/// `st_move` do not need it: they confirm their result with `path_exists`, so
/// the message only ever reaches the user when something actually failed.
pub fn st_from_oem( s: Str) -> Str {
    if !st_is_windows() {
        return s
    }
    if !st_has_high_byte(s) {
        return s
    }
    mut parts: List<Str> = []
    let n = byte_len(s)
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let b = str_byte_at(s, i)
        if b < 128 {
            parts = parts.push(str_substring(s, i, 1))
        } else {
            if b == 255 {
                parts = parts.push(" ")
            } else {
                parts = parts.push(st_char_idx(st_oem_table(), b - 128))
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return str_join(parts, "")
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ platform

pub fn st_is_windows() -> Bool {
    // Measured, not assumed: `env_get("OS")`, `ComSpec` and `SHELL` all come
    // back empty in this runtime, while `SystemRoot` returns "C:\\Windows".
    // Windows always sets it and Unix never does, so it is the reliable signal.
    if byte_len(env_get("SystemRoot")) > 0 {
        return true
    }
    return st_has_infix(env_get("OS"), "Windows")
}

/// Every path in this module is stored with forward slashes.
///
/// Measured: mixing separators produced `D:/IDE datara\ryan-harness`, which
/// Windows tolerates but which then travelled into `/api/tree` and `/api/read`
/// as a different string from the one the user picked. Forward slashes work on
/// Windows too, so one separator everywhere removes the whole class of problem.
pub fn st_slash( p: Str) -> Str {
    return str_replace(p, "\\", "/")
}

/// Is this `X:/` - a drive root and not a directory on a drive?
pub fn st_is_drive_root( s: Str) -> Bool {
    if byte_len(s) != 3 {
        return false
    }
    if str_substring(s, 1, 1) != ":" {
        return false
    }
    return str_substring(s, 2, 1) == "/"
}

/// Normalise a user-supplied path into the one form the rest of the code uses.
///
/// The `X:` case is the important one: a bare drive letter names the drive's
/// *current directory*, so `D:` and `D:/` are different places and `/` is not
/// the empty string. A trailing separator is stripped from ordinary paths but
/// never from a root.
pub fn st_norm_path( p: Str) -> Str {
    let s0 = st_slash(str_trim(p))
    if byte_len(s0) == 0 {
        return "."
    }
    if byte_len(s0) == 2 {
        if str_substring(s0, 1, 1) == ":" {
            return s0 + "/"
        }
    }
    mut s = s0
    while byte_len(s) > 3 && st_has_suffix(s, "/") {
        s = str_substring(s, 0, byte_len(s) - 1)
    }
    return s
}

/// The directory above `path`, or the path itself at a root.
///
/// There is no `str_rfind`, so the parent is found by scanning forward and
/// keeping the last separator seen. The drive case is why this is not one line:
/// the parent of `D:/ryan` is `D:/`, not `D:`.
pub fn st_parent_of( p: Str) -> Str {
    let norm = st_norm_path(p)
    if norm == "/" || norm == "." || st_is_drive_root(norm) {
        return norm
    }
    mut last = -1
    let n = byte_len(norm)
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        if str_substring(norm, i, 1) == "/" {
            last = i
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if last <= 0 {
        return "/"
    }
    let head = str_substring(norm, 0, last)
    if byte_len(head) == 2 {
        if str_substring(head, 1, 1) == ":" {
            return head + "/"
        }
    }
    return head
}

/// A short name for the folder, for the dialog title.
pub fn st_base_of( p: Str) -> Str {
    let norm = st_norm_path(p)
    if st_is_drive_root(norm) {
        return str_substring(norm, 0, 2)
    }
    if norm == "/" {
        return "/"
    }
    let n = byte_len(norm)
    mut last = -1
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        if str_substring(norm, i, 1) == "/" {
            last = i
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if last < 0 {
        return norm
    }
    let tail = str_substring(norm, last + 1, n - last - 1)
    if byte_len(tail) == 0 {
        return norm
    }
    return tail
}

/// Join with a forward slash on every platform.
pub fn st_join( a: Str, b: Str) -> Str {
    let norm = st_slash(a)
    if st_has_suffix(norm, "/") {
        return norm + b
    }
    return norm + "/" + b
}

/// The same path in backslashes.
///
/// Needed because of a measured asymmetry, and only for the shell commands that
/// are left. `dir`, `cd`, `mkdir` and `type` all accept forward slashes, so the
/// whole codebase uses them. But `move`, `ren` and `del` do **not** - with a
/// forward slash they print "Системе не удается найти указанный путь." and fail.
/// Tested both ways:
///
///   move "D:\...\c.txt" "D:\...\d.txt"   -> Перемещено файлов: 1.   OK
///   move "D:/.../d.txt" "D:/.../e.txt"   -> путь не найден          fails
///
/// Rather than converting the whole codebase to backslashes - which is how the
/// mixed-separator bug started - the conversion is confined to the call sites
/// that need it.
pub fn st_back_path( p: Str) -> Str {
    return str_replace(p, "/", "\\")
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- native filesystem

/// Does `path` name an existing directory? **Takes a filesystem path.**
///
/// `file_exists` returns false for a folder (measured), and `path_exists`
/// answers true for a folder, a file, a missing path and a drive root alike
/// (measured on 1.4.0: `D:/` is true). So the two together are exactly the test,
/// with no process launch.
///
/// The path must be in the form the filesystem uses - see `st_list_names` for
/// why that is not the same as the form a person reads. Callers holding a path
/// from the browser run it through `st_fs_path` first.
pub fn st_dir_exists( path: Str) -> Bool {
    let p = st_norm_path(path)
    if byte_len(p) == 0 {
        return false
    }
    mut present = false
    mut is_file = false
    unsafe(justification: "the explorer must tell a folder from a file") {
        present = path_exists(p)
        if present {
            is_file = file_exists(p)
        }
    }
    return present && !is_file
}

/// Does `path` name an existing file? **Takes a filesystem path.**
pub fn st_file_exists( path: Str) -> Bool {
    let p = st_norm_path(path)
    if byte_len(p) == 0 {
        return false
    }
    mut r = false
    unsafe(justification: "the explorer must check whether the file exists") {
        r = file_exists(p)
    }
    return r
}

/// A Datara `Bool` as the JSON literal.
///
/// Lives here rather than in `layout.dtr` because everything below this module
/// needs it, and `layout` imports `explorer` - the reverse would be a cycle.
pub fn st_bool( v: Bool) -> Str {
    if v {
        return "true"
    }
    return "false"
}

/// Entry names inside `dir`, **raw**, exactly as the runtime handed them over.
///
/// Raw, not UTF-8, and that is the whole design of this module. The runtime's
/// filesystem builtins are ANSI on Windows in *both* directions - `dir_list`
/// returns cp1251 (`FindFirstFileA`, `datara_runtime.c:2623`) and
/// `path_exists`/`file_exists`/`file_read` expect cp1251 back. Measured: a path
/// spelled in UTF-8 for the folder `тест папка 123` answers `false`, while the
/// same path built from the cp1251 bytes answers `true`.
///
/// So a name has two lives, and mixing them is the bug this module exists to
/// avoid:
///
///   * **raw** - what the filesystem understands. Use it to join, to test and
///     to descend.
///   * **`st_from_ansi(raw)`** - what a person reads. Use it only when the value
///     is about to become JSON.
///
/// A client path arrives in UTF-8, so anything that takes one from the wire goes
/// through `st_fs_path` first, which turns it back into raw. That function is a
/// no-op for the ASCII paths that are almost all of them.
pub fn st_list_names( dir: Str) -> List<Str> {
    mut raw: List<Str> = []
    unsafe(justification: "the explorer lists the folder the user is browsing") {
        raw = dir_list(st_norm_path(dir))
    }
    return raw
}

/// The raw entry name inside `parent` whose readable form is `want`, or "".
///
/// This is how a UTF-8 path from the browser is turned back into something the
/// filesystem accepts: the name is *found* in the parent's own listing rather
/// than reconstructed, because a Datara string cannot be built from byte values
/// and cp1251 has no arithmetic relationship to UTF-8.
fn st_raw_name_for( parent: Str, want: Str) -> Str {
    let names = st_list_names(parent)
    let n = names.len()
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let raw = names[i]
        i = i + 1
        if st_from_ansi(raw) == want {
            return raw
        }
    }
    return ""
}

/// Turn a path from the browser (UTF-8) into a path the filesystem accepts.
///
/// Free for ASCII, which is the case that matters for speed: one scan finds no
/// high byte and the string is returned untouched. Only a segment that actually
/// contains one costs a directory read.
///
/// Returns "" when a segment cannot be found, so a caller reports "not found"
/// rather than inventing a path.
pub fn st_fs_path( p: Str) -> Str {
    let norm = st_norm_path(p)
    if !st_has_high_byte(norm) {
        return norm
    }
    let parts = str_split(norm, "/")
    let n = parts.len()
    // the first segment is the root: "D:" for "D:/x", empty for "/x"
    mut cur = "/"
    mut i = 0
    if n > 0 && byte_len(parts[0]) > 1 {
        cur = parts[0]
        i = 1
        if i < n && byte_len(parts[i]) == 0 {
            cur = cur + "/"
            i = i + 1
        }
    } else {
        i = 1
    }
    while i < n {
        let seg = parts[i]
        i = i + 1
        if byte_len(seg) == 0 {
            continue
        }
        if !st_has_high_byte(seg) {
            cur = st_join(cur, seg)
            continue
        }
        let raw = st_raw_name_for(cur, seg)
        if byte_len(raw) == 0 {
            return ""
        }
        cur = st_join(cur, raw)
    }
    return cur
}

/// The one honest sentence for a name the runtime cannot be handed.
///
/// A path that is being *created* does not exist yet, so it cannot be found in
/// its parent's listing and its raw spelling cannot be recovered. Everything
/// except the last segment resolves the normal way; the last segment is usable
/// only while it stays ASCII, because a Datara string cannot be built from byte
/// values and cp1251 has no arithmetic relationship to UTF-8.
///
/// So a new folder whose name is not ASCII is refused with a reason rather than
/// created under a mojibake name, which is what handing UTF-8 to a shell that
/// reads cp1251 used to do.
pub fn st_no_bytes_msg() -> Str {
    return "this name leaves ASCII, and a name that does not exist yet cannot be "
         + "spelled for the filesystem: the runtime builds paths in the ANSI "
         + "codepage and a Datara string cannot be built from raw bytes"
}

/// Like `st_fs_path`, but for a path that may not exist yet.
///
/// Free for ASCII. Otherwise the parent is resolved against the filesystem and
/// the last segment is appended only when it is ASCII. Returns "" when that is
/// impossible, so the caller reports it instead of inventing a name.
pub fn st_fs_create_path( p: Str) -> Str {
    let norm = st_norm_path(p)
    if !st_has_high_byte(norm) {
        return norm
    }
    let name = st_base_of(norm)
    if byte_len(name) == 0 || st_has_high_byte(name) {
        return ""
    }
    let parent = st_fs_path(st_parent_of(norm))
    if byte_len(parent) == 0 {
        return ""
    }
    return st_join(parent, name)
}

/// Directory names that are never worth showing.
///
/// Only the ones that are unambiguously generated and can hold tens of
/// thousands of entries. `dist` and `build` are deliberately *not* here: they
/// are often real folders someone made on purpose, and hiding a folder the user
/// created is worse than showing one they did not.
pub fn st_skip_name( name: Str) -> Bool {
    if name == "node_modules" {
        return true
    }
    if name == "target" {
        return true
    }
    if name == ".git" {
        return true
    }
    if name == "__pycache__" {
        return true
    }
    if name == ".venv" {
        return true
    }
    if name == "venv" {
        return true
    }
    if name == ".forgen_cache" {
        return true
    }
    if name == ".next" {
        return true
    }
    if name == ".cache" {
        return true
    }
    if name == ".mypy_cache" {
        return true
    }
    return name == ".pytest_cache"
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- the tree

/// How deep the walk goes before it stops. A real project is far shallower.
fn st_max_depth() -> Int => 10

/// How many entries the walk collects before it stops.
fn st_max_entries() -> Int => 6000

/// Walk `dir` and return one flat list of entries.
///
/// Each element is `"D" + path` for a folder or `"F" + path` for a file, so a
/// single return value carries both and the recursion needs no shared mutable
/// state. The budget is checked **before** descending, which is the whole point:
/// the first version handed `dir /b /s` a drive root and had to wait for the
/// disk to be enumerated before any cap could apply.
fn st_walk( root: Str, budget: Int) -> List<Str> {
    mut acc: List<Str> = []
    // Breadth first, and that is the point of the shape. A depth-first walk
    // spends its whole budget inside the first folder it meets, so opening a
    // drive root showed 5998 files and 2 folders - measured - because it had
    // descended into one branch and never come back. Level by level, the top of
    // the tree is always complete and only the deep end is cut off.
    mut level: List<Str> = [root]
    mut d = 0
    while d <= st_max_depth() {
        mut next: List<Str> = []
        let ln = level.len()
        mut li = 0
        while li < ln {
            let dir = level[li]
            li = li + 1
            let names = st_list_names(dir)
            let n = names.len()
            mut i = 0
            while i < n {
                let name = names[i]
                i = i + 1
                if st_skip_name(name) {
                    continue
                }
                if acc.len() >= budget {
                    return acc
                }
                let full = st_join(dir, name)
                if st_dir_exists(full) {
                    acc = acc.push("D" + full)
                    next = next.push(full)
                } else {
                    acc = acc.push("F" + full)
                }
            }
        }
        level = next
        if level.len() == 0 {
            return acc
        }
        d = d + 1
    }
    return acc
}

/// The workspace, flat: every file and every folder.
///
/// Flat rather than a tree because the UI builds a tree from paths in ten lines
/// of JavaScript, and a flat list is also exactly what a file picker wants.
///
/// **Folders are listed, not just files.** The first version ran `dir /b /s /a-d`
/// (the `/a-d` means "not directories"), so a folder could never appear in the
/// explorer at all - creating one appeared to do nothing. Both files and folders
/// now come from one walk.
///
/// The resolved root is returned, so the interface can show names relative to
/// it instead of a `D:` / `IDE datara` ladder at the top of the tree. (It used to
/// be three rungs - `D:` / `ryan` / `IDE datara` - because the studio sat inside
/// the forgen_ai project; moving it to the drive root took one rung out.)
///
/// **A drive root is no longer a special case.** `dir /b /s` from `D:/` walked
/// the whole disk - measured at 3.9 GB - so the old code listed a drive root one
/// level deep and said `shallow: true`. `st_walk` checks its budget before every
/// descent, so a drive root is walked exactly as far as the budget allows and
/// stops. The `shallow` flag is kept in the response because the interface shows
/// it, and it is now simply "the walk hit its budget".
pub fn st_tree( root: Str) -> Str {
    let asked = str_trim(root)
    let shown = st_norm_path(asked)
    let base_dir = st_fs_path(asked)
    if !st_dir_exists(base_dir) {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"root\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
             + ",\"files\":[],\"dirs\":[]"
             + ",\"error\":\"cannot read that folder\""
             + ",\"detail\":\"not a folder: " + st_json_escape(shown) + "\"}"
    }

    let all = st_walk(base_dir, st_max_entries())

    mut files = "["
    mut dirs = "["
    let n = all.len()
    mut fcount = 0
    mut dcount = 0
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let entry = all[i]
        i = i + 1
        if byte_len(entry) < 2 {
            continue
        }
        let kind = str_substring(entry, 0, 1)
        let path = str_substring(entry, 1, byte_len(entry) - 1)
        if path == base_dir {
            continue
        }
        // raw on the inside, readable on the wire
        let shown_path = st_from_ansi(path)
        if kind == "D" {
            if dcount > 0 {
                dirs = dirs + ","
            }
            dirs = dirs + "\"" + st_json_escape(shown_path) + "\""
            dcount = dcount + 1
        } else {
            if fcount > 0 {
                files = files + ","
            }
            files = files + "\"" + st_json_escape(shown_path) + "\""
            fcount = fcount + 1
        }
    }
    files = files + "]"
    dirs = dirs + "]"

    return "{\"ok\":true,\"root\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
         + ",\"asked\":\"" + st_json_escape(asked) + "\""
         + ",\"files\":" + files + ",\"dirs\":" + dirs
         + ",\"file_count\":" + int_to_str(fcount)
         + ",\"dir_count\":" + int_to_str(dcount)
         + ",\"shallow\":" + st_bool(n >= st_max_entries())
         + ",\"truncated\":" + st_bool(n >= st_max_entries()) + "}"
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ browsing

/// Directories inside `path`, as JSON, for the folder dialog.
///
/// Never invents an entry: an unreadable folder comes back with `ok:false` and
/// a readable reason, because a browser that shows a folder called "Системе не
/// удается найти указанный путь." is worse than one that admits it could not
/// open the folder.
///
/// `parent` and `roots` are returned even on failure, so the dialog always has
/// somewhere to go.
pub fn st_list_dir( path: Str) -> Str {
    // what the browser asked for, in the form it understands
    let shown = st_norm_path(path)
    // what the filesystem needs
    let base_dir = st_fs_path(path)

    let head = ",\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
             + ",\"name\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_base_of(shown)) + "\""
             + ",\"parent\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_parent_of(shown)) + "\""
             + ",\"roots\":" + st_roots_json()
             + ",\"windows\":" + st_bool(st_is_windows())

    if !st_dir_exists(base_dir) {
        return "{\"ok\":false" + head
             + ",\"count\":0,\"dirs\":[],\"files\":[]"
             + ",\"error\":\"cannot open that folder\""
             + ",\"detail\":\"not a folder: " + st_json_escape(shown) + "\"}"
    }

    let names = st_list_names(base_dir)
    let n = names.len()
    mut dirs = "["
    mut files = "["
    mut dcount = 0
    mut fcount = 0
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let name = names[i]
        i = i + 1
        if name == "." || name == ".." || byte_len(name) == 0 {
            continue
        }
        // classify by the RAW name, because that is what the filesystem
        // accepts; only the values that reach the browser are converted
        let full = st_join(base_dir, name)
        let shown_full = st_join(shown, st_from_ansi(name))
        let item = "{\"name\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_from_ansi(name)) + "\",\"path\":\""
                 + st_json_escape(shown_full) + "\"}"
        if st_dir_exists(full) {
            if dcount > 0 {
                dirs = dirs + ","
            }
            dirs = dirs + item
            dcount = dcount + 1
        } else {
            if fcount < 300 {
                if fcount > 0 {
                    files = files + ","
                }
                files = files + item
                fcount = fcount + 1
            }
        }
    }
    dirs = dirs + "]"
    files = files + "]"

    return "{\"ok\":true" + head
         + ",\"count\":" + int_to_str(dcount)
         + ",\"dirs\":" + dirs
         + ",\"files\":" + files + "}"
}

/// Every drive that actually exists, as a JSON array.
///
/// Twenty-six `path_exists` probes rather than a shell loop. `path_exists` is
/// true for a drive root (measured: `D:/` is true), and it is one native call
/// with no process launch, so the old `for %d in (A B C ...) do @if exist %d:\`
/// line and its quoting are gone.
pub fn st_roots_json() -> Str {
    if !st_is_windows() {
        return "[\"/\"]"
    }
    mut arr = "["
    mut count = 0
    let letters = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ"
    let n = byte_len(letters)
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let root = str_substring(letters, i, 1) + ":/"
        i = i + 1
        mut present = false
        unsafe(justification: "the folder dialog must offer the drives that exist") {
            present = path_exists(root)
        }
        if present {
            if count > 0 {
                arr = arr + ","
            }
            arr = arr + "\"" + root + "\""
            count = count + 1
        }
    }
    arr = arr + "]"
    return arr
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ mutation
//
// Three operations have no builtin in forgen 1.4.0, so they go through the
// shell - and each one then confirms its result by looking at the filesystem,
// because the shell's success message is translated and the translation is not
// ours to guess.

/// Create a directory, parents included.
///
/// `path` comes from the browser, so it is UTF-8 and goes through
/// `st_fs_create_path` before anything touches the filesystem. `shown` is the
/// same path in the form the browser understands, and it is what every reply
/// echoes: the raw spelling is an implementation detail of this module and the
/// interface must never see it.
pub fn st_mkdir( path: Str) -> Str {
    let shown = st_norm_path(path)
    if byte_len(shown) == 0 || shown == "." {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"error\":\"empty path\"}"
    }
    let target = st_fs_create_path(shown)
    if byte_len(target) == 0 {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
             + ",\"error\":\"cannot create that folder\""
             + ",\"detail\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_no_bytes_msg()) + "\"}"
    }
    return st_mkdir_do(target, shown)
}

/// The body of `st_mkdir`, for callers that already hold a filesystem path.
///
/// `st_move` and `st_new_file` resolve their destination once and pass the raw
/// form down, so this takes both spellings rather than converting twice.
fn st_mkdir_do( target: Str, shown: Str) -> Str {
    if st_dir_exists(target) {
        return "{\"ok\":true,\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\"}"
    }

    mut res = ""
    if st_is_windows() {
        // `mkdir` is a cmd builtin on Windows, not a process that `exec` can
        // start by name. Call cmd.exe explicitly, just like move and delete.
        res = st_sh("mkdir \"" + st_back_path(target) + "\" 2>&1")
    } else {
        res = st_sh("(mkdir -p \"" + target + "\") 2>&1")
    }

    if st_dir_exists(target) {
        return "{\"ok\":true,\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\"}"
    }
    return "{\"ok\":false,\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
         + ",\"error\":\"could not create the folder\""
         + ",\"detail\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_from_oem(str_trim(res))) + "\"}"
}

/// Move or rename a file or a folder.
///
/// The destination's folder is created first when it is missing, so dragging a
/// file into a folder that does not exist yet does the obvious thing instead of
/// failing with a shell message.
///
/// The parameters are not called `from` and `to`: `from` is a reserved word and
/// using it is a parse error, not a warning.
pub fn st_move( src_path: Str, dst_path: Str) -> Str {
    // both arrive from the browser in UTF-8; `shown` is what the reply echoes,
    // the resolved pair is what the filesystem and the shell are given
    let a_shown = st_norm_path(src_path)
    let b_shown = st_norm_path(dst_path)
    if byte_len(a_shown) == 0 || byte_len(b_shown) == 0 {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"error\":\"empty path\"}"
    }
    if a_shown == b_shown {
        return "{\"ok\":true,\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(b_shown) + "\",\"moved\":false}"
    }

    let a = st_fs_path(a_shown)
    let b = st_fs_create_path(b_shown)
    if byte_len(a) == 0 || byte_len(b) == 0 {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"from\":\"" + st_json_escape(a_shown) + "\""
             + ",\"to\":\"" + st_json_escape(b_shown) + "\""
             + ",\"error\":\"cannot move to that name\""
             + ",\"detail\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_no_bytes_msg()) + "\"}"
    }
    if !st_file_exists(a) && !st_dir_exists(a) {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"from\":\"" + st_json_escape(a_shown) + "\""
             + ",\"error\":\"nothing to move there\"}"
    }
    if st_file_exists(b) || st_dir_exists(b) {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"to\":\"" + st_json_escape(b_shown) + "\""
             + ",\"error\":\"something is already there\"}"
    }

    let parent = st_parent_of(b)
    if byte_len(parent) > 0 && parent != "." && !st_dir_exists(parent) {
        let made = st_mkdir_do(parent, st_from_ansi(parent))
        if !st_has_infix(made, "\"ok\":true") {
            return "{\"ok\":false,\"to\":\"" + st_json_escape(b_shown) + "\""
                 + ",\"error\":\"could not create the destination folder\"}"
        }
    }

    // `move` is one of the three commands that reject forward slashes
    // `move` is a cmd builtin, not an executable. `exec` starts a process
    // directly on Windows, so the bare command silently leaves the source in
    // place. Invoke cmd.exe explicitly and keep both paths quoted.
    let res = st_sh("move \"" + st_back_path(a) + "\" \"" + st_back_path(b) + "\" 2>&1")

    // confirmed by looking, not by reading the shell's message
    if st_file_exists(b) || st_dir_exists(b) {
        return "{\"ok\":true,\"from\":\"" + st_json_escape(a_shown) + "\""
             + ",\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(b_shown) + "\",\"moved\":true}"
    }
    return "{\"ok\":false,\"from\":\"" + st_json_escape(a_shown) + "\""
         + ",\"to\":\"" + st_json_escape(b_shown) + "\""
         + ",\"error\":\"could not move it\""
         + ",\"detail\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_from_oem(str_trim(res))) + "\"}"
}

/// Create a file, making its folder first when that folder does not exist.
///
/// `file_write` cannot create a directory, so typing `tools/check.dtr` used to
/// fail with no explanation when `tools` was absent. Making the parents here
/// means the name the user typed is the name they get.
pub fn st_new_file( path: Str, content: Str) -> Str {
    let shown = st_norm_path(path)
    if byte_len(shown) == 0 || shown == "." {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"error\":\"empty path\"}"
    }
    let target = st_fs_create_path(shown)
    if byte_len(target) == 0 {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
             + ",\"error\":\"cannot create that file\""
             + ",\"detail\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_no_bytes_msg()) + "\"}"
    }

    let parent = st_parent_of(target)
    if byte_len(parent) > 0 && parent != "." && !st_dir_exists(parent) {
        let made = st_mkdir_do(parent, st_from_ansi(parent))
        if !st_has_infix(made, "\"ok\":true") {
            return "{\"ok\":false,\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
                 + ",\"error\":\"could not create the folder "
                 + st_json_escape(st_from_ansi(parent)) + "\"}"
        }
    }

    unsafe(justification: "the explorer must create the file the user asked for") {
        file_write(target, content)
    }

    if st_file_exists(target) {
        return "{\"ok\":true,\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
             + ",\"bytes\":" + int_to_str(byte_len(content)) + "}"
    }
    return "{\"ok\":false,\"path\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
         + ",\"error\":\"could not create the file\"}"
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- search

/// Extensions worth searching, as findstr masks.
///
/// A fixed list rather than "everything": a content search over a workspace that
/// includes `node_modules` or a `target` directory spends all its time in files
/// nobody wrote.
fn st_search_masks() -> Str {
    return "\"*.dtr\" \"*.md\" \"*.toml\" \"*.txt\" \"*.json\" \"*.js\" \"*.mjs\""
         + " \"*.ts\" \"*.rs\" \"*.py\" \"*.sh\" \"*.html\" \"*.css\" \"*.c\" \"*.h\""
         + " \"*.cpp\" \"*.hpp\" \"*.yml\" \"*.yaml\" \"*.gitignore\""
}

/// Content search across the workspace.
///
/// `findstr /s /n /i /c:"query"` prints `path:line:text` and is fast enough to
/// run on every Enter in the search box. The query is rejected if it contains a
/// quote, because that would end the `/c:` argument and turn a search into
/// whatever the rest of the string happened to be.
///
/// `whole` restricts the hits to lines where the query appears as a complete
/// name. It is filtered here rather than in the shell because `findstr` needs
/// `/r` to express a word boundary and `/r` also turns the query into a regular
/// expression - so a search for `a.b` would become a pattern and a search for
/// `count` would need escaping. Doing it on the returned line text is one
/// implementation, identical on both platforms, and testable.
///
/// The filter is **case-sensitive** even though the search itself is not. That
/// looks inconsistent and is deliberate: `whole` exists for symbols, and in
/// Datara `Count` and `count` are two different names, so a case-insensitive
/// whole-word match would answer "everywhere this variable is used" with places
/// it is not used. Free-text search leaves `whole` off and keeps the case
/// insensitivity that makes it useful for prose.
pub fn st_find( root: Str, query: Str, whole: Bool) -> Str {
    let q = str_trim(query)
    if byte_len(q) == 0 {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"error\":\"empty query\"}"
    }
    if st_has_infix(q, "\"") {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"error\":\"quotes are not allowed in a search\"}"
    }
    let shown = st_norm_path(root)
    let base_dir = st_fs_path(shown)
    if byte_len(base_dir) == 0 || !st_dir_exists(base_dir) {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"root\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
             + ",\"hits\":[],\"count\":0"
             + ",\"error\":\"search failed\""
             + ",\"detail\":\"not a folder\"}"
    }

    mut listing = ""
    if st_is_windows() {
        // `cd` and the `&&` chain are cmd syntax. `exec` does not invoke a
        // command shell implicitly, so use cmd.exe explicitly just as the
        // filesystem move/mkdir paths do.
        listing = st_sh("cd /d \"" + st_back_path(base_dir) + "\" && echo " + st_ok_mark()
                        + " && cd && findstr /s /n /i /c:\"" + q + "\" " + st_search_masks()
                        + " 2>&1")
    } else {
        listing = st_sh("(cd \"" + base_dir + "\" && echo " + st_ok_mark()
                        + " && pwd && grep -rn -i --include=\"*.dtr\" --include=\"*.md\""
                        + " --include=\"*.toml\" --include=\"*.txt\" --include=\"*.js\""
                        + " --include=\"*.rs\" --include=\"*.py\" -e \"" + q + "\" .) 2>&1")
    }

    if !st_has_infix(listing, st_ok_mark()) {
        return "{\"ok\":false,\"root\":\"" + st_json_escape(shown) + "\""
             + ",\"hits\":[],\"count\":0"
             + ",\"error\":\"search failed\""
             + ",\"detail\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_from_oem(st_first_line_of(listing))) + "\"}"
    }

    // line 0 after the marker is the resolved root, echoed by `cd` / `pwd`, so
    // the hits come back absolute whatever the caller passed in
    let all = st_lines_after_marker(listing)
    let resolved = all[0]

    mut arr = "["
    mut kept = 0
    let n = all.len()
    mut i = 1
    while i < n && kept < 400 {
        let raw = all[i]
        i = i + 1
        let parts = str_split(raw, ":")
        // windows: path:line:text   unix grep -rn: ./path:line:text
        if parts.len() < 2 {
            continue
        }
        let file = parts[0]
        let num = parts[1]
        if byte_len(file) == 0 || byte_len(num) == 0 || !st_is_number(num) {
            continue
        }
        let text = st_after_n_colons(raw, 2)
        let full = st_abs(st_slash(file), resolved)
        let clean = st_from_oem(st_strip_ansi(str_trim(text)))
        // The whole-word test runs on the decoded text, not on the raw shell
        // bytes: the search is case-insensitive and `findstr` will happily match
        // inside a name, which is what this is here to undo.
        if whole && !st_has_word(clean, q) {
            continue
        }
        if kept > 0 {
            arr = arr + ","
        }
        arr = arr + "{\"file\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_from_oem(full)) + "\""
                 + ",\"line\":" + num
                 + ",\"text\":\"" + st_json_escape(clean) + "\"}"
        kept = kept + 1
    }
    arr = arr + "]"
    return "{\"ok\":true,\"root\":\"" + st_json_escape(st_from_oem(resolved)) + "\""
         + ",\"query\":\"" + st_json_escape(q) + "\""
         + ",\"whole\":" + st_bool_json(whole)
         + ",\"count\":" + int_to_str(kept) + ",\"hits\":" + arr + "}"
}

/// `true` / `false` as JSON, since there is no boolean formatter.
pub fn st_bool_json( b: Bool) -> Str {
    if b {
        return "true"
    }
    return "false"
}

/// True when `c` can appear inside a name: `[A-Za-z0-9_]`.
///
/// ASCII on purpose. Datara identifiers are ASCII, so this is the compiler's own
/// definition of a name boundary - and it has to be a byte test, because the
/// line may contain UTF-8 comments and treating every continuation byte as a
/// name byte would make a search inside a Cyrillic comment behave differently
/// from the same search inside an ASCII one.
fn st_is_name_byte( c: Int) -> Bool {
    if c >= 48 && c <= 57 {
        return true
    }
    if c >= 65 && c <= 90 {
        return true
    }
    if c >= 97 && c <= 122 {
        return true
    }
    return c == 95
}

/// True when `line` contains `q` as a complete name rather than inside a longer
/// one.
///
/// This is the difference between "find this variable everywhere" and "find
/// everything that starts with this variable". Searching `stock` and being shown
/// `release_stock`, or searching `count` and being shown `counter` and
/// `discount`, is how a reference list turns into something nobody reads.
///
/// Case-sensitive - see the note on `st_find`.
pub fn st_has_word( line: Str, q: Str) -> Bool {
    let ln = byte_len(line)
    let qn = byte_len(q)
    if qn == 0 || qn > ln {
        return false
    }
    mut i = 0
    mut found = false
    while i + qn <= ln && !found {
        if str_substring(line, i, qn) == q {
            mut before_ok = i == 0
            if i > 0 {
                before_ok = !st_is_name_byte(str_byte_at(line, i - 1))
            }
            mut after_ok = i + qn >= ln
            if i + qn < ln {
                after_ok = !st_is_name_byte(str_byte_at(line, i + qn))
            }
            if before_ok && after_ok {
                found = true
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return found
}

/// True when every byte of `s` is a digit and it is not empty.
fn st_is_number( s: Str) -> Bool {
    if byte_len(s) == 0 {
        return false
    }
    let n = byte_len(s)
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let c = str_substring(s, i, 1)
        if !st_has_infix("0123456789", c) {
            return false
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}

/// Everything after the `skip`-th colon.
///
/// `str_split` cannot be used for the text, because the matched line usually
/// contains colons of its own and splitting then rejoining would collapse runs
/// of them.
fn st_after_n_colons( s: Str, skip: Int) -> Str {
    let n = byte_len(s)
    mut seen = 0
    mut i = 0
    while i < n && seen < skip {
        if str_substring(s, i, 1) == ":" {
            seen = seen + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if i >= n {
        return ""
    }
    return str_substring(s, i, n - i)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- shell text

/// Make a listed path absolute.
///
/// Windows `dir /s` already prints absolute paths; `findstr /s` prints paths
/// relative to the folder we entered, so the folder has to go back in front.
/// This lives here rather than in `api.dtr` because path arithmetic belongs in
/// one module - and `api` already depends on `explorer`, so the reverse would
/// have been a cycle.
pub fn st_abs( p: Str, root: Str) -> Str {
    let s = st_slash(p)
    if st_has_prefix(s, "/") {
        return s
    }
    if byte_len(s) > 1 && str_substring(s, 1, 1) == ":" {
        return s
    }
    mut rel = s
    if st_has_prefix(rel, "./") {
        rel = str_substring(rel, 2, byte_len(rel) - 2)
    }
    return st_join(root, rel)
}

/// Lines that follow the success marker, trimmed, empties dropped.
///
/// The marker is what makes a shell call's outcome readable without reading its
/// error text, and everything after it is the payload.
pub fn st_lines_after_marker( raw: Str) -> List<Str> {
    let lines = str_split(raw, "\n")
    mut kept: List<Str> = []
    let n = lines.len()
    mut i = 0
    mut seen = false
    while i < n {
        let t = str_trim(lines[i])
        i = i + 1
        if !seen {
            if st_has_infix(t, st_ok_mark()) {
                seen = true
            }
            continue
        }
        if byte_len(t) > 0 {
            kept = kept.push(t)
        }
    }
    return kept
}

/// The first non-empty line of a failed command, for the error message.
pub fn st_first_line_of( s: Str) -> Str {
    let lines = str_split(s, "\n")
    let n = lines.len()
    mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let t = str_trim(lines[i])
        i = i + 1
        if byte_len(t) > 0 && !st_has_infix(t, st_ok_mark()) {
            return t
        }
    }
    return ""
}

/// The directory holding the `datara.toml` that governs `path`, or `""`.
///
/// A project-wide check has to be aimed at the project. Measured on 1.4.0:
/// `forgen check` on a directory that contains two sibling projects merges them
/// into one namespace and reports `E-RESOLVE-002: Duplicate function definition`
/// for every name they share - two files called `helper`, two `main` - so a
/// folder that is not itself a project cannot be checked.
///
/// Walks up from `path` (or from its directory when `path` is a file) and stops
/// at the first `datara.toml`. `within` bounds the search: when it is not empty
/// the walk never goes above it, so a check aimed at a workspace root can never
/// escape into an unrelated project that happens to sit above it. That is not
/// hypothetical - it is what happened while the studio lived at
/// `D:/ryan/IDE datara`, where walking up landed on `D:/ryan` and its forgen_ai
/// `datara.toml`. The studio has since moved to `D:/IDE datara`, which has no
/// project file above it, so the bound is no longer load-bearing here - but it
/// is kept, because the studio is installed wherever a person puts it and the
/// bound is what makes the answer independent of that. The loop is bounded as
/// well as root-terminated, because a path cycle would otherwise spin the server.
pub fn st_project_root( path: Str, within: Str) -> Str {
    // the walk is done in the filesystem's own spelling and converted once at
    // the end, because the answer is shown to a person
    let limit = st_fs_path(st_norm_path(within))
    mut dir = st_fs_path(st_norm_path(path))
    if byte_len(dir) == 0 {
        return ""
    }
    if !st_dir_exists(dir) {
        dir = st_parent_of(dir)
    }
    let bounded = byte_len(str_trim(within)) > 0 && byte_len(limit) > 0
    mut guard = 0
    while guard < 64 {
        if st_file_exists(st_join(dir, "datara.toml")) {
            return st_from_ansi(dir)
        }
        let up = st_parent_of(dir)
        if up == dir {
            return ""
        }
        if bounded && dir == limit {
            return ""
        }
        dir = up
        guard = guard + 1
    }
    return ""
}
